{{\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Scientific Debugging
\par}
{\tc\tcl2 \v Scientific Debugging}
The application includes the 'sample' program of the 'scientific debugging' part, enabled with the {\f2 {\b PL_CONFIG_USE_SAMPLE}}  setting in `platform.h.\par
Open a serial terminal (Termite/PuTTy or the 'Serial Monitor' in VS Code). Connect to the VCOM (115200, CRLF at the end). Type in 'help' to get some guidance. Example in using Serial Monitor in VS Code: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid ---- Opened the serial port COM181 ----\par
---- Sent utf8 encoded message: "help\\r\\n" ----\par
Sample program. Type in numbers, separated by spaces. Press <ENTER> at the end.\par
---- Sent utf8 encoded message: "20 5 3\\r\\n" ----\par
Output: 3 5 20 \par
}
\par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAAKX}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAAKX}
{{\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Doxygen
\par}
{\tc\tcl2 \v Doxygen}
Run task: Menu Terminal -> Run Task... -> doxygen\par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAAKY}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAAKY}
{{\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Coverage
\par}
{\tc\tcl2 \v Coverage}
Enable {\f2 {\b PL_CONFIG_USE_GCOV}}  in {\f2 {\b src/platform.h}}  and use the {\f2 Debug}  build. Run the application with the debugger.\par
Note: with DevContainer, the current directory of the GDB server needs to be the {\b project root folder} : {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid JLinkGDBServerCL -select USB -device RP2040_M0_0 -endian little -if SWD -rtos GDBServer/RTOSPlugin_FreeRTOS\par
}
\par
If using DevContainer, you have to install {\f2 gcovr}  with {\f2 pip} . First we need to create a new local python environment in a folder ({\f2 penv} ): {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid cd /workspace\par
python3 -m venv penv\par
./penv/bin/pip install gcovr\par
}
 After that, one can use 'gcovr' with the python {\f2 penv}  environment: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid ./penv/bin/gcovr .\par
}
\par
To generate a text report (use {\f2 ./penv/bin/gcovr}  with a virtual environment): {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid gcovr .\par
}
 To generate a HTML report: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid gcovr --html-details -o ./gcovr/main.html\par
}
 or use Terminal -> Run Task... -> gcovr\par
You can use the {\f2 gcov}  utility on a file like this: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid gcov build/debug/srcLib/CMakeFiles/srcLib.dir/main.c.gcda\par
}
\par
To see coverage in VS Code: {\f2 ctrl} +{\f2 shift} +{\f2 p} : {\f2 Gcov Viewer: Show} \par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAAKZ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAAKZ}
{{\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Testing
\par}
{\tc\tcl2 \v Testing}
Build with {\f2 Test}  build configuration. Disable {\f2 {\b PL_CONFIG_USE_GCOV}}  in {\f2 {\b src/platform.h}} .\par
If using DevContainer, you {\i have}  to use an IP connection to the debug probe/server on the host. For this, start a GDB server on the host for debugging ({\f2 JLinkGDBServerCLExe}  on Linux): {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid JLinkRemoteServerCL\par
}
 The file {\f2 src/tests/CMakeLists.txt}  uses an environment variable {\f2 PICO_SEGGER_IP}  (if defined). This is the IP address of the host with the J-Link debug probe. You can set that IP address in {\f2 .devcontainer\\devcontainer.env} .\par
Manual Test Runner run (e.g. Windows host with J-Link attached), from the project root folder: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid JRun --device RP2040_M0_0 --pc off --sp off --if SWD --args "Led_1" build/Test/TSM_PicoW_Blinky.elf\par
}
 To run it from a DevContainer with the address of the host: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid JRun --device RP2040_M0_0 --pc off --sp off --if SWD --ip "192.168.65.254" --args "Led_1" build/Test/TSM_PicoW_Blinky.elf\par
}
 Manual CTest run: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid ctest --extra-verbose --test-dir build/Test --timeout 15 --output-on-failure -R Led_1\par
}
\par
Otherwise, you have to use a tunnel service: {
\par
\pard\plain \s40\li0\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid JLinkRemoteServerCL -UseTunnel -TunnelBySN -select="801048148"\par
}
 \par}
}}
